ontext also creates space for strange myths and legends to emerge from the shadows.<br /> <br /> I'm thinking of the wrathful deities, who might be defending local Buddhist and non-Christian populations from the predations of Kindred (they look like this: <a href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Wrathful_deities\" target=\"_blank\">https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Wrathful_deities</a>). That could be the Wan Kuei trying to strike back and survive. I can see online that a version of the rakshasa (luocha) existed in Siberian Buddhist mythology, which could be Ravnos or illusion-spinning/shapeshifting Wan Kuei.<br /> <br /> There would be a population of dispossed and oppressed people (mainly the indigenous peoples of Buryatia, nearby Tuva, and Kalmykia) among whom the Wan Kuei might hide and prey, in order to protect their own interests, such as dragon's nest, ancestor worship that feeds them and their spirit allies, and so on. There might even be some cool forbidden Buddhist items lost to the expulsions which could form cool story hooks. See info on Buddhist relics here: <a href=\"https://&amp;quot;https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Relics_associated_with_Buddha&amp;quot;\" target=\"_blank\">https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Relics...ed_with_Buddha</a><br /> <br /> ETA1: This link also looks useful (behind a paywall but you get a number of articles for free, I think?): <a href=\"https://tricycle.org/magazine/buddhism-russia/\" target=\"_blank\">https://tricycle.org/magazine/buddhism-russia/</a><br /> <br /> ETA2: Key Buddhist sites: <a href=\"https://&amp;quot;https://www.rbth.com/travel/330675-buddhist-sites-russia&amp;quot;\" target=\"_blank\">https://www.rbth.com/travel/330675-b...t-sites-russia</a></div> </div> </div> </div>I was definitely thinking on having a look to Wind from the East, not that much because of the Buddhist angle as from the Mongol one. My understanding is that the Cossacks, who actually had a significant role in the war both in the southern and eastern fronts, may have been strongly influenced by Mongol and Tatar culture, so I am thinking of having some remains of the Anda bloodline still present in the conflict","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":2}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/27429-microcuchon","name":"Microcuchon","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"May 2022"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"201"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2022-07-27T10:54:44-07:00","id":1488186,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Microcuchon</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/vampire-the-masquerade/1487731-st-vault-idea-research?p=1488178#post1488178\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">I was definitely thinking on having a look to Wind from the East, not that much because of the Buddhist angle as from the Mongol one. My understanding is that the Cossacks, who actually had a significant role in the war both in the southern and eastern fronts, may have been strongly influenced by Mongol and Tatar culture, so I am thinking of having some remains of the Anda bloodline still present in the conflict</div> </div> </div> </div>Yeah, there's probably enough for its own book, so pick what fits, I think.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>Writer, publisher, performer<br /> Mostly he/his, sometimes she/her IRL <a href=\"https://adam-lowe.com\" target=\"_blank\">https://adam-lowe.com</a>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[